# Q-034 Layer C demonstration: corpus 17 with Lambda effects ELIDED.
#
# The canonical corpus/17-builtin-compose-pure-and-effectful.json has
# no top-level Lambda — it's just a top-level Application of `add` to
# `(now(), 1)`. To demonstrate Layer C effect-closure inference, we wrap
# the same expression in a no-arg Lambda whose declared effects field
# is omitted. The Elaborator should walk the body, see the
# `Application(now, [])` whose callee declares `[timeFx]`, and fill in
# `effects = [timeFx]` on the Lambda before the dag-json is emitted.
#
# This is a 1-way compilation: the elaborated dag-json verifies cleanly
# but does NOT hash-equal a hand-authored equivalent that explicitly
# declares effects (the Lambda would still have the same effects, but
# the canonical form for this elided-effects program is whatever
# elaboration produces).
@v=1 root=app
intT PRM Int
timeFx EFC "Time.Now"
nowT FNT [] intT
now FN "strand-builtin:Time.Now" nowT [timeFx]
addT FNT [intT intT] intT
add FN "strand-builtin:Int.Add" addT
callNow APP now []
one ILT 1
addedExpr APP add [callNow one]
# Lambda with NO effects argument — elaborator fills in [timeFx] based
# on the body's closure (the Application of `now` releases [timeFx]).
myLam LAM [] addedExpr
app APP myLam []
